    "whyStatic":  "Adding a path seam to SecretStore would touch credential code, which I had flagged as needing confirmation. SaveCore write contract is source-decidable, so I pinned it with static guards (same approach as A6/A13) instead - no authorization needed.",
    "contract":  "SaveCore performs exactly 7 SecretStore.Save calls: per-account token; clear per-account legacy key; clear global MicrosoftRefreshToken; write CurseForgeApiKey from config; write ProxyPassword from config; clear global AiApiKey; write per-provider key.",
    "whyClearsMatter":  "The three clears are the tail of the migration from global keys to per-account/per-provider keys. Missing one leaves BOTH the old and new key on the user machine, and the old key may still be read elsewhere - an inconsistency.",
    "extraCheck":  "Test 3 catches literal-vs-constant drift: ConfigStore uses literals while SecretStore uses constants; if a constant is renamed and the literal is not, credentials get written to a key nobody reads (silent failure). Test 4 pins the three key names because they are part of the on-disk format - renaming orphans credentials already saved on user machines.",
    "redBaseline":  "deleting the global AiApiKey clear produced 3 failures (tests 1, 2 and 3)",
